; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; parser — ported from the reference's internal/parser/parser.go.
;
; SLICES 1–13c. The spine, the declaration router, the TYPE surface, the
; SIGNATURE, the EXPRESSION ladder, the CLASS family, the TYPE-MEMBER surface,
; the IMPORT/EXPORT declarations, the loop and branch statements, the binding
; pattern, the template family, the DECORATOR and the ERROR ROUTE. Anything
; outside them reports its own POSITION as unported rather than building a
; plausible tree that quietly disagrees with the reference — the same mechanism
; the scanner carries, and for the same reason.
;
; ── What is parsed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
;   slice 1  SourceFile, EndOfFile
;            EmptyStatement, Block, ExpressionStatement
;            VariableStatement / VariableDeclarationList / VariableDeclaration
;            Identifier, NumericLiteral, BigIntLiteral, StringLiteral, and the
;            keyword expressions this / super / null / true / false
;   slice 2  modifiers and the declaration ROUTER — every declaration form goes
;            through parse_declaration, because modifiers are parsed before
;            anyone knows what they modify
;   slice 3  the type parser (see the TYPES banner far below), the type ALIAS
;            declaration, and `: T` type annotations
;   slice 4  the SIGNATURE (see the SIGNATURES banner): parameter lists, return
;            types and type predicates, the function declaration, and the
;            function and constructor TYPES the type parser was waiting on
;   slice 5  the EXPRESSION ladder (see the EXPRESSIONS banner), and with it
;            three things that only became REACHABLE once expressions parsed:
;            `await`/`using` at statement start (both fall through to an
;            expression statement when the look-ahead says no declaration), the
;            HasJSDoc clear on a `(`-headed expression statement, and the
;            top-level-await REPARSE condition — the reference's second pass
;            over statements that used `await` as a name in a module
;   slice 6  the CLASS family (see the CLASSES banner): the declaration and the
;            expression, the heritage clauses, and the six member forms —
;            method, property, constructor, get/set accessor, static block,
;            index signature. Three of those entry points are SHARED with the
;            object literal, so `{ m() {} }` and `{ get p() {} }` land here too;
;            and the private name `#x` comes with them, because a private name
;            exists only inside a class body
;   slice 7  the TYPE-MEMBER surface (see the TYPE MEMBERS banner): the object
;            type literal and the five member forms an interface body is made
;            of, the mapped type, and the interface, enum and module
;            declarations — one production seen from two places
;   slice 8  the IMPORT and EXPORT declarations (see the IMPORTS AND EXPORTS
;            banner), which are parseDeclarationWorker's last two arms: with
;            them the declaration ROUTER slice 2 bought is complete
;   slice 9  the LOOP and BRANCH statements — the remaining arms of
;            parseStatement, six independent ports sharing only the `for` head
;   slice 10 the BINDING PATTERN, and with it skip_parameter_start's ANSWER
;   slice 11 the TEMPLATE family — the expression, the literal TYPE, the tagged
;            form, and the scanner's ReScanTemplateToken all three wait on
;   slice 12 the DECORATOR (see the DECORATORS banner at parse_modifiers): the
;            node, the two runs parseModifiersEx admits it in, and
;            parseDecoratedExpression, the `@` arm of the primary expression.
;            It is the LAST construct the unported histogram could name — what
;            remains behind it is the diagnostics DECISION and a tail of
;            one-and-two-case tags.
;   slice 13c the ERROR ROUTE and the LIST RECOVERY — the decision the histogram
;            had been naming for four columns. The parser REPORTS now: ~99
;            parseErrorAt* sites, the two missing-node factories
;            (parseExpectedToken and createIdentifierWithDiagnostic, plus
;            createMissingList), the missing-semicolon guesser with its keyword
;            spell-checker, and the recovery proper — a `parsing_contexts`
;            bitset, is_list_element's in_error_recovery parameter,
;            abortParsingListOrMoveToNextToken and parsingContextErrors' 18
;            arms. The scanner's remaining numeric reports come with it, because
;            an UNDER-report is an UNEXPLAINED failure once the rest is compared
;   slice 14 the DECLARATION-FILE flag: a file's NAME is an input to its parse,
;            so IsDeclarationFileName makes the whole file AMBIENT before the
;            first token. Its sliver of internal/tspath is tspath.scaly
;   slice 15 the top-level-await REPARSE — the reference's SECOND PASS. Slice 5
;            ported the CONDITION and reported the construct; this parses the
;            recorded statements again with AwaitContext on, re-slicing the
;            diagnostics around them and rewinding after each span. It is the
;            last construct in this port that had a route behind it
;   slice 16 the REGULAR EXPRESSION literal: the scanner's ReScanSlashToken and
;            the `/` arm of the primary expression, which is the whole of it on
;            this side. The last re-scan entry point outside JSDoc and JSX
;   slice 21 the JSDOC grammar, at the bottom of this file. A third token
;            grammar, a truncated source text, and the port's third YARDSTICK —
;            JSDoc is not in the tree the parser one walks, so neither existing
;            instrument could see this slice in either direction. It also
;            supplies the last three re-scan entry points,
;            ReScanAsteriskEqualsToken, ReScanQuestionToken and ReScanHashToken,
;            which closes that family
;
; ★ THE LIST ABOVE STOPS AT 21 AND SEVEN SLICES ARE MISSING FROM IT — 17 (the
;   stored token VALUE, which is the scanner's), 18 (the import EXPRESSION and
;   type), 19 (the JSON grammar, a second ENTRY POINT), 20 (the JSX grammar), 22 (the JSDoc
;   REPARSER, which makes JSDoc the TYPE SYNTAX of a .js file), 23 (the comment
;   PRAGMAS) and 24 (checkJSSyntax, whose diagnostics the reference keeps out of
;   sf.Diagnostics()). Each is written up in CLAUDE.md, at the banner of the code
;   it added, or both; naming the gap here is worth more than a summary nobody
;   wrote at the time and nobody can now reconstruct exactly. Said plainly rather
;   than left to be noticed, which is what this section is for.
;
; ── What it deliberately does not, each stated rather than left to be noticed ─
;
; ★ DIAGNOSTICS ARE PORTED (slice 13c), and the entry that stood here — "this
;   file's own ~99 sites still emit none, so any path that would emit one
;   reports unported instead" — is retired. What survives it is the reason it
;   was written: a parser that recovers from an error without producing the
;   error is a parser whose tree cannot be compared, and one that invents a code
;   is worse. Both are now avoided by REPORTING rather than by stopping.
;
;   ★★ The consequence for the negative controls INVERTS with it. Through slice
;   12 the port bailed at every point the reference would RECOVER at, so most
;   ways of breaking it on purpose surfaced as an extra UNPORTED rather than as
;   a wrong tree, and roughly sixty controls in CLAUDE.md's tables were gated by
;   the matched COUNT rather than by a red. Those become real reds as the
;   fixtures behind them start being compared — which is what this slice was
;   worth, since it buys correctness evidence and not cases.
;
; ★★★ THE HISTOGRAM HAD NOTHING LEFT TO NAME (after slice 12) — and its LAST
;   ROW WAS THREE QUARTERS AN ARTEFACT, which slice 13a measured and removed
;   without touching this file. It read: of 40 corpus first blockers the
;   DIAGNOSTICS slice is 28 (70 %). But a corpus case is a HARNESS SCRIPT, and
;   the runner used to feed the whole concatenation to both parsers, so 209 of
;   the 241 syntactic diagnostics the reference produced over the corpus came out
;   of package.json and tsconfig.json bodies read as TypeScript — 25 of those 34
;   cases were blocked ONLY by that. Splitting each case with the reference's own
;   splitter took the corpus from 256 to 283 fully-matched cases and the corpus
;   diagnostics to FIFTEEN over six units. See CLAUDE.md §3.5ac; the transferable
;   half is that an instrument reporting where the port STOPS is exact about that
;   and says nothing about whether the input deserved to be parsed.
;
;   ★★ So the diagnostics slice is now three corpus units — and is still the
;   right next port, for the reason the case counts never showed: 34 of the 38
;   unported FIXTURES are diagnostic-bearing, and roughly sixty
;   matched-count-only gates in CLAUDE.md's control tables become real reds only
;   once this port emits diagnostics. It buys correctness evidence, not cases.
;
;   ★★ AND IT IS A SPLIT OF `unported`, NOT A REPLACEMENT — this header used to
;   say "replacing the whole `unported` mechanism", which is wrong. The sites
;   standing in for p.diagnostics become diagnostics; the ~40 standing for a
;   construct nobody built stay unported. ★★★THAT LIST IS EMPTY AS OF SLICE 25,
;   and every entry it ever held is named here with the slice that retired it,
;   because the rule below is what the list was for: `toplevel-await-reparse`
;   (slice 15), `regex` (16), `import-expression` (18, which built the construct),
;   JSX (20), JSDoc (21), the JSDoc REPARSER (22) and `list-comma` (13c, where it
;   became a diagnostic rather than a construct — read off `git log -S`, not
;   assumed). What the ladder itself still omits is named at the
;   EXPRESSIONS banner, one line per construct. ★An entry naming a construct
;   nobody has built is a claim with an expiry date, and the slice that builds it
;   OWNS THE LINE — this list carried JSX and JSDoc for four slices past their
;   ports, which is the same lapse §3.5bu catalogues on the scanner's side.
;
; ★ ERROR RECOVERY IS PORTED (slice 13c). Every list — the source file's own
;   statement list included — runs through parse_list/parse_delimited_list with
;   a parsing CONTEXT, and a token that starts no element is offered to the
;   enclosing lists before it is skipped. The note that stood here said the
;   recovery "only runs after a diagnostic, and a diagnostic is already an
;   unported report", which was true and is exactly what expired.
;
;   ★★ One shape is worth knowing because it cost five corpus units to find:
;   a routine that answers NULL without setting the unported record produces
;   `UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0` from the dumper, which reads like an honest report of
;   a missing construct and is a PORT DEFECT. It arrived when parse_semicolon
;   stopped stopping — seventeen callers still read its result as failure — and
;   the tag is the only thing that distinguishes the two.
;
; ── Three mapping decisions, all for CLAUDE.md §3 ───────────────────────────
;
; (1) `lookAhead(callback)` becomes a method per call site that marks, scans and
;     rewinds inline. Go passes a func value; Scaly's only escaping-closure form
;     is `return \x: …`, and a speculation helper taking a callback is exactly
;     the shape the escape checker exists to reject. The saved block is
;     ScannerState — which is the whole reason the scanner keeps one (§3.5).
;
; (2) The reference's `switch p.token` becomes a run of INDEPENDENT `if` blocks
;     that return, never an `else if` chain: a chain nests one planner level per
;     arm at roughly 110 KB of planner stack per arm under the bootstrap stages,
;     and this switch has about forty. The arms are disjoint by construction —
;     each tests the same token against a distinct kind — so the two are exactly
;     equivalent here. Same reasoning as the scanner's, one level up.
;
; (3) `unported` has TWO records since slice 5, because the reference's `rewind`
;     truncates its diagnostics and ours has to have the same lifetime. Which
;     record a site writes says what its report means — see the ParserState
;     header, which is where the argument lives.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.String
use tscaly.scanner.Scanner
use tscaly.scanner.ScannerState
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList
use tscaly.Diagnostics.Diagnostic
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.ast.NodeData
use tscaly.ast.TokenData
use tscaly.ast.IdentifierData
use tscaly.ast.LiteralData
use tscaly.ast.EmptyStatementData
use tscaly.ast.SourceFileData
use tscaly.ast.BlockData
use tscaly.ast.ExpressionStatementData
use tscaly.ast.VariableStatementData
use tscaly.ast.VariableDeclarationListData
use tscaly.ast.VariableDeclarationData
use tscaly.ast.ReturnStatementData
use tscaly.ast.IfStatementData
use tscaly.ast.WrappedTypeData
use tscaly.ast.TypeListData
use tscaly.ast.QualifiedNameData
use tscaly.ast.TypeReferenceData
use tscaly.ast.TypeQueryData
use tscaly.ast.IndexedAccessTypeData
use tscaly.ast.ConditionalTypeData
use tscaly.ast.TypePredicateData
